ck like that  and then when you've had a response the  one and the likes of which we've done  i think any of those other chancellors  probably have ever had to do as much  fiscal support for  the country as i have had to i i think  it's reasonable to expect that what we  have to do in response to all of those  things is going to be a bit different  to what's happened in the past but what  i would say is  what are we doing what are we doing with  those taxes what we're doing is funding  strong public services  people elected us because we told them  we were the party of public services we  were going to invest in the nhs in  schools  and we were also going to invest in  projects across the country to level up  opportunity  that's what we want to get on and  deliver during this parliament and we  also want to be responsible with the  public finances for the reasons i set  out  and it's because of those reasons that  i've take taken the two decisions i've  made today  and i do believe they are the right  decisions for the country  and for the economy  next we're going to helia  okay that's it  good afternoon chancellor the obr points  out  that in your calculations there's no  provision for  virus-related costs beyond next year  despite the fact that the government  acknowledges that there will be an  annual vaccination program there'll be  further costs  associated with test and trace as well  as of course the nhs increased cost  because of cancelled operations and  procedures  you've made a lot today about wanting to  be honest with people  do you think it's your is it your honest  judgment  today that the pandemic will cost  nothing from next year  on public spending you're right to  differentiate between the  the general plans we have uh which i can  go through in a second and then also the  plans for coronavirus and it's right  think about those things differently now  obviously at this moment  we don't know exactly the future path  of what's going to happen but w