ng on  constitutional questions at the moment i  think what people want is us to be  focusing on our response  to the pandemic making sure that we get  the vaccine rolled out  as quickly as we can into as many  people's arms as possible  and then with that with the prime  minister's roadmap  cautiously and safely reopen our economy  get our lives back to normal and try and  protect as many jobs as possible  and i think that's actually what people  want us to focus on  which is what we're getting on and doing  with regard to the universal credit  uplift  i announced today that we are extending  that for six months uh this was  something that we put in place  temporarily at the beginning of the  pandemic when we were in lockdown  and the six months extension means that  it will be in place  well beyond the end of this current  lockdown  and it only forms one part of our  response to helping those on low incomes  or those who are vulnerable  there's lots of other things we're doing  helping people with their council tax  bills  with their rental payments through  something called local housing allowance  but crucially the best way to help  people over time  is to give them the skills they need to  get great jobs or get better jobs  which is why we've increased the  national living wage next year  above inflation which means a 350 pound  pay rise for those on low incomes  and we're also with the prime minister's  lifetime skills guarantee for example  giving every adult the opportunity to go  and get a government-funded  qualification  at a good standard which will help them  increase their prospects of getting a  job or getting a new job  and they're doing lots of other things  to encourage companies to hire people  for example our kickstart scheme for  young people or indeed  the 3000 pounds that we're paying  companies to take on a new apprentice  so there's lots of different ways to  help people the figures and the facts in  the document today show that those on  th