ht also want to have  remediation work done  so what we're doing there for those  smaller buildings is providing a  government-backed loan scheme  which will provide low-cost  government-backed loans to people  which will be capped at no more than 50  pounds a month  so that will help provide funds to deal  with those buildings  and also we know that there have been  some problems with the mortgage market  and people asking for particular forms  so we're working very hard the housing  secretary the prime minister and i  with mortgage lenders and the industry  to make sure that the use of those forms  they're called ews one forms  that have been causing the issue the use  of those forms is going to be  proportionate  and we really hope that will help  unblock the market together  uh with a government insurance scheme  that we're providing for people who do  those assessments  so i think the combination of five  billion pounds of funding to remedy to  remediate the most unsafe buildings  a cheap government loan to help with the  rest and these interactions  interventions on the mortgage market  when you put them all together  hopefully they will make an enormous  difference and provide reassurance to  people  through what i know has been a difficult  process  so next if we could go please  yes uh hello chancellor sizable islam  um chancellor what would you say to the  i think it's million  also people that will enter the income  tax system the million or so people that  will  end up uh in the higher rate  and when you put that together with the  uh rise in corporation tax this is a  turning round of  conservative principles has the pandemic  fundamentally changed the conservative  approach to  big business big borrowing big taxation  and big spending  thanks faisal well hopefully both in the  remarks i made  earlier now and in the house of commons  earlier people will have understood the  logic what we're trying to do  now obviously the pandemic has dealt us  uh  pretty bad damage to the public finances  in the economy and i think  i and the government have a  responsibility to put those on back in a  strong position over time but do that in  a way  that is supportive our economic recovery  in the short term but also do so in a  way that is fair  and i think both of the measures that  we've implemented the ones that you  mentioned  past that test for example that the  threshold freeze  means that those on the lowest incomes  contribute much less than those  on higher incomes and it's something  that only builds over time so it's very  supportive  of our recovery and importantly nobody's  current take-home pay  will be affected as a result of this  policy  what this policy does is mean that in  the future  any benefit that might have come from  thresholds rising with inflation  will not be there and that's what will  help us rebuild our public finances in a  fair  and honest way and i know that's been  supported by several think tanks and  others who have said that's a sensible  thing to do  and with grant a corporation tax we  remain a party that believes in a  government that believes in supporting  business  and which is why our corporation tax  even after this change  will still be the lowest out of the g7  large economies that we  compete with so i think that's something  that we will be proud to do and make  sure that we support business in that  way but also  crucially we will be exempting the vast  majority of businesses are small and  medium-sized businesses  from that increase because we want those  with the bigger shoulders just the  largest most profitable country at  companies  to make the contribution in two years  time and also  there are lots of other things in  today's budget which is supportive of  business  whether that's short-term support with  business rates  holidays vat cuts cash grants  but also new help to grow management and  digital incentives  because we want to help small and  medium-sized businesses