e that  signals  uh that britain is is open for business  and supportive  of business and as i was saying to  faisal the super deduction  the tax cut for business that we've put  in place over the next two years to  support the recovery  to drive up business investment in new  factories new machines  across the country that's an enormous  tax cut for business  i think it's the biggest business tax  cut that i think we've ever seen in  british history as far as we can  calculate  but i think that shows we're on the side  of business and we know they can help  play their part in the recovery but i  also think it's reasonable given the  amount of support that we've  given business given the the crisis we  face with our public finances  that it's reasonable to ask those  largest  most successful companies in two years  time to contribute  uh something back to help us rebuild i  think that's a reasonable and fair  approach  that will support us in the long term  but it's reasonable in the  in the long term as well  i think next are we going to ed  yes hello hi chancellor ed conway news  can you can you hear me  and behind me we've got a chart that  we've been looking at a lot  uh today at sky news showing you tax as  a percentage of gdp basically the tax  burden  uh in the uk and i suppose my question  is you know looking at this chart and  looking at how  high the tax burden is getting towards  the end of the forecast horizon  are you concerned about being the  chancellor with the highest  tax burden of any chance of going all  the way back to the 1960s  and roy jenkins does that concern you at  all  well ed i guess what the chart your  chart doesn't show is  all the other chancellors if any of them  have had pandemics to deal with  and you know we haven't had a pandemic  like this in over 100 years  and so i think that's remember that's  why we're having this conversation  that's the problem that we're grappling  with and  unsurprisingly you know when you've had  a shock like that  and then when you've had a response the  one and the likes of which we've done  i think any of those other chancellors  probably have ever had to do as much  fiscal support for  the country as i have had to i i think  it's reasonable to expect that what we  have to do in response to all of those  things is going to be a bit different  to what's happened in the past but what  i would say is  what are we doing what are we doing with  those taxes what we're doing is funding  strong public services  people elected us because we told them  we were the party of public services we  were going to invest in the nhs in  schools  and we were also going to invest in  projects across the country to level up  opportunity  that's what we want to get on and  deliver during this parliament and we  also want to be responsible with the  public finances for the reasons i set  out  and it's because of those reasons that  i've take taken the two decisions i've  made today  and i do believe they are the right  decisions for the country  and for the economy  next we're going to helia  okay that's it  good afternoon chancellor the obr points  out  that in your calculations there's no  provision for  virus-related costs beyond next year  despite the fact that the government  acknowledges that there will be an  annual vaccination program there'll be  further costs  associated with test and trace as well  as of course the nhs increased cost  because of cancelled operations and  procedures  you've made a lot today about wanting to  be honest with people  do you think it's your is it your honest  judgment  today that the pandemic will cost  nothing from next year  on public spending you're right to  differentiate between the  the general plans we have uh which i can  go through in a second and then also the  plans for coronavirus and it's right  think about those things differently now  obviously at this moment  we don't know exactly the future path  of what's going to happen but what we do  know is that this year there's an  enormous amount of call  for extra funding for chronovirus which  is why why today i announced  1.6 billion pounds of extra funding for  example  for the vaccination program to make sure  that we can get that  get that all the resources it needs to  happen as quickly as possible  provide for potential booster shots but  also to improve our preparedness  for future pandemics so some of the  things we're doing is to build that  capacity to react  quickly in the future so i don't have a  crystal ball about about next year but  we are thinking  about the next 12 months and what's  required you talked about nhs backlog  at the spending review in november we  announced billions of pounds of extra  funding  for the nhs both for diagnostic  equipment to  upgrade some of the older diagnostic  equipment and to provide more ability  for the nhs to do elective surgery this  year to start catching up on some of the  backlog that we know exists  but to your broader question about  public services and public spending  the numbers that are set out today show  that there will be strong growth  strong investment in public services not  just over these two years but for the  remainder of the parliament  as well and next  i think catherine sampson yes thank you  chancellor adjust your reaction please  the two points  uh first of all scotland's finance  secretary claims you've not matched her  ambition for supporting economic  recovery and jobs and she's especially  disappointed you didn't make that uplift  to universal credit  and permanence just your reaction to  that and secondly  your business minister suggested an  interview yesterday when it came to an  independence referendum  it was up to the scottish people to  decide when and whether they want one  do you agree with him and is the  treasury planning some change of  strategy here  well catherine i you know i i genuinely  believe that i don't think people want  any of us to be focusing on  constitutional questions at the moment i  think what people want is us to be  focusing on our response  to the pandemic making sure that we get  the vaccine rolled out  as quickly as we can into as many  people's arms as possible  and then with that with the prime  minister's roadmap  cautiously and safely reopen our economy  get our lives back to normal and try and  protect as many jobs as possible  and i think that's actually what people  want us to focus on  which is what we're getting on and doing  with regard to the universal credit  uplift  i announced today that we are extending  that for six months uh this was  something that we put in place  temporarily at the beginning of the  pandemic when we were in lockdown  and the six months extension means that  it will be in place  well beyond the end of this current  lockdown  and it only forms one part of our  response to helping those on low incomes  or those who are vulnerable  there's lots of other things we're doing  helping people with their council tax  bills  with their rental payments through  something called local housing allowance  but crucially the best way to help  people over time  is to give them the skills they need to  get great jobs or get better jobs  which is why we've increased the  national living wage next year  above inflation which means a 350 pound  pay rise for those on low incomes  and we're also with the prime minister's  lifetime skills guarantee for example  giving every adult the opportunity to go  and get a government-funded  qualification  at a good standard which will help them  increase their prospects of getting a  job or getting a new job  and they're doing lots of other things  to encourage companies to hire people  for example our kickstart scheme for  young people or indeed  the 3000 pounds that we're paying  companies to take on a new apprentice  so there's lots of different ways to  help people the figures and the facts in  the document today show that those on  the lowest incomes  have been supported the most by all the  interventions that we've put in place  over the last 12 months so i'm proud of  that  that is this government acting to  protect those who really need our help  but as the economy reopens it's right  that we transition our support to  focusing on people  getting them into work getting them the  skills they need and that's what this  budget delivers  next i think we're going over to the  times  and philip aldrich phillip you there yes  hi  chancellor um uh on  on corporation tax the uh you you're  undoing  10 years of tax policy by  raising corporation tax up to 25 in 2023  uh and you're even reintroducing a small  business tax rate so  i just wondered um was the policy the  previous policy  wrong um and and also what gives you  confidence that your tax rises are not  going to choke off the recovery in 2023  there's still spare capacity under the  obr's forecasts  right at the end of the forecast period  so it looks like  um there's more to go for in in terms of  growth  thanks phil i've been on on i think it's  not right to judge decisions that were  made over the last 10 years  uh by the fact that today we're dealing  with the results of a  hopefully once in a century pandemic  that we're grappling with the decisions  that were made in the past were right  for them  and we're making the right decisions for  now i think with corporation tax over  time  you know what's transpired is it might  not be the most effective way  to drive capital investment up which is  what we want to see because we want  businesses to invest  to create jobs and new machinery to  drive up productivity and  we think our super deduction uh  hopefully will do a better job of that  over the next two years  um and that and i think we'll see we'll  hopefully see the results  of that and then with regard to your  second question  we need obviously to be sensitive about  the timing of this to make sure that we  do support the recovery in t