e capacity funding  to bid for projects  is based on an index of economic need uh  which is  transparently uh published i think  actually by mhclg  and based on a bunch of objective  measures so that will be there for  people  to have a look at and remember that's  only areas that have received  some capacity funding to bid no area is  excluded for bidding  it's just that those areas on the basis  of this formula might need a bit of  extra help so we're giving those local  areas  some money to put their bid together to  help them uh and  and you know to your broader point i  think if you look at all the things  we're doing they're benefiting people  in every corner of the country so we for  example announced the location of our  eight free ports in england  today you know they're spread in eight  english regions  uh in it from southampton to t side to  liverpool  you know across the country and all of  those areas are actually represented by  a mix  of both mps and local authorities  because what we're interested in is  making sure that wherever you grow up  wherever you happen to live  that this government is helping to  provide opportunity for you  and creating jobs in your area and i  firmly believe that whether it's the  leveling up fund  or our free ports initiative or any  other things we're doing you know we are  delivering and making good on that  promise  next we're going to graham from the  daily mirror graham you there  i am hope you can hear me yes i can hi  good evening hello good afternoon um are  you  chancellor are you prepared to extend  further the cut-off date for the  renewed support that you've announced  today if and while no one wants this the  timing for the roadmap  um has to change and then secondly if i  may what do you say to those who have  labelled it an insult  that there's no pay rise for many  thousands  of public sector workers who've been uh  on the front line during the  june pandemic there's been no pay rise  announced by you today  uh graham to your first point what we've  done very deliberately  is extend all of our support well beyond  the end of the road map which you know  the prime minister set out some dates in  in april march april may and then  finally june  for example look at furlough which is  being extended all the way sept to  september  in line with many other things the  reason we've taken that approach is as i  said in the  in the house of commons today is because  we want to make sure that we've made  allowance for even the most cautious  possible interpretation of the road map  of what might happen  um so we've done that in advance to  provide people with that  certainty uh up front so i think i think  we're well well protected on that and  that i think has been met broadly uh  well today by stakeholders to your  second point on public sector pay  this was something that i addressed in  the spending review  back in november when we talked about  pay for the next year  and back then what i said which i'm  happy to to go over now is  if i when i was looking at all the  numbers what we saw was  in the private sector wages were falling  people were having their hours cut many  of them were furloughed  and sadly almost three-quarters of a  million people in private sector had  lost their jobs  and almost none of those things had  happened in the public sector  so given that and given the obviously  very difficult fiscal situation  situation that we face that we've been  talking about you know i thought for  reasons of fairness  and also to try and protect those public  sector jobs  it was reasonable to take a more  targeted approach to public sector pay  for this next year  so what does that mean that means those  who work in the nhs  a million people it's about a quarter of  all people in the public sector  they will receive a pay rise next year  and we're all very grateful to them  for the fantastic work that they are  doing under difficult conditions  but for everyone else 